Optimal Solution - Use 1D dynamic programming to check if a subset with sum equal to half of the total array sum exists.
                    This is a 0/1 knapsack problem where we check if any subset sums to half of the total array sum.

class Solution {
    public boolean canPartition(int[] nums) {
        // Calculate total sum of the array
        int sum = Arrays.stream(nums).sum();
        // If total sum is odd, it cannot be split into two equal subsets
        if (sum % 2 != 0)
            return false;
        int target = sum / 2;
        // dp[i] = true if a subset with sum i is achievable
        boolean[] dp = new boolean[target + 1];
        // Base case: sum 0 is always achievable (empty subset)
        dp[0] = true;
        // Process each number
        for (int num : nums) {
            // Traverse backwards to avoid reusing the same number
            for (int i = target; i >= num; i--) {
                // If we can make (i - num), then we can make i
                if (dp[i - num]) {
                    // Early exit if target is reached
                    if (i == target)
                        return true;
                    dp[i] = true;
                }
            }
        }
        // Final result: whether target sum is achievable
        return dp[target];
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(n x s).
Space Complexity :- O(s). s = total sum of elements
